# const found = (i: number) => pairs.get(rows[i & 7]!.id + "," + rows[i & 3]!.id);
# A number beside a written text surely fits in one string, so nobody can tell whether that join is made. Its only reader stands behind reads that may run code, in another block.
# Where the reader's text is the key of a Map the earlier join of two parts is a key itself: each of the two is found in the cache of pairs by the bits of its parts, and the second finds the first as the same cell. (One key of three parts, with the earlier join taken apart, went through an operation: 595 | 438 instructions a call where the keys come again, 1,499 | 1,230 where they are new.)
